WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.810
- If some big industrial
company or research lab

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:05.970
wants to prototype a new road surface
or road building method,

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.930
how do they test that for the long term?

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.440
Roads need to last decades.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.190
How do they simulate hundreds
of thousands of vehicles

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:14.410
rolling over their new road for years,

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:16.440
without actually doing that?

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:17.820
The answer is something called

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.130
an "accelerated pavement testing facility".

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.570
And there's only a few in the world.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.760
This is one of the largest.
It was built in 1984.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:24.890
It's in the west of France.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.950
And it's better known
as the fatigue carousel.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:31.080
- The fatigue carousel
consists of a central tower,

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.810
with hydraulic motors
which run the machine.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.610
And then it has four arms,

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.410
and outer wheels which load the pavements.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:41.420
A ring is 120 metres in length.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.750
And the test section is six metre wide.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.920
So this machine allows to
simulate, in a few months,

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.720
10 or 20 years of
traffic on the real road.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:51.960
For a long period,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.120
the machine was used to validate
French pavement materials.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.930
And now we are trying to
find new applications.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.900
Recently, we had a test
with bio-binders,

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:03.300
to replace bitumen,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.920
because petroleum will,
in some time, disappear.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.380
And now there is also a lot of interest,

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.193
for charging electric
vehicles by the road.

00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:14.520
[machine hums]

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.300
- You can tell how heavy those arms are,

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.830
by how much those tyres
squash at the bottom. Oh--

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.884
[machine noise, increasing in pitch]

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:26.470
It's a good noise.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.380
- The tests are done
with the outer wheels,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.310
at loads corresponding to heavy vehicles.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.638
But we can also install on
each arm,

00:01:35.638 --> 00:01:40.220
single wheels, dual wheels or also
two axles or three axles.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.800
When the machine runs,

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:47.290
we can also move the wheels laterally,
to reproduce the effect of real traffic.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:48.840
And you can also change the loads.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.840
On a single axle, we can go from
4.5 tonnes to about 8.5 tonnes.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:59.143
So sometimes we can overload the pavement,
to accelerate deterioration.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.950
- It takes a few minutes
to creep up to speed.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:06.520
And it's kind of a little
bit unnerving standing here.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.200
Because, it started off as,

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.673
"oh, it's very slow.
oh, this is all very safe."

00:02:11.673 --> 00:02:15.653
And now it's-- now there's a lot of
metal coming towards me!

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.670
Faster and faster.

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:22.820
- This machine can run
at about 100km/h.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:26.130
The usual testing speed
is 70km/h,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.070
because we don't want
to go to the maximum,

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:31.260
but we can go to 100km/h.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.292
- I thought this was up to speed.

00:02:33.292 --> 00:02:35.192
And I think it's still getting faster.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.387
I've got dust in my eye from the wind. Ah!

00:02:40.336 --> 00:02:43.840
- We instrument, usually, the soil
and also the pavement layers.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.080
So in the soil we put, generally,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.500
our displacement transducers,
our vertical strain transducers,

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.510
to measure the vertical displacement.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.430
And then in the bituminous layers,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.580
the most common instrumentation
is strain gauges.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.680
We use, also temperature sensors,

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.870
because temperature
variation is very important,

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:02.650
for the pavement response.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:05.750
And we use, also,
accelerometers or geophones,

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.560
to measure the vertical acceleration,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.210
and then to get the vertical displacement.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.570
- Testing on this carousel

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:14.130
doesn't mean just for a
few hours or a few days.

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:17.740
A test here can last months,
the carousel constantly rotating,

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.550
with the wheels travelling at anything
up to 100km/h,

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.020
with a thousand-horsepower
engine powering them,

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.530
so that years' worth of pavement
stress and road damage

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:26.800
can be built up in weeks.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.450
There are actually three different
circular test tracks here,

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.370
and the carousel can be taken
apart and moved between them.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.810
That way, while a test is happening
over here for a few weeks,

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.948
another track can be torn up and rebuilt
for the next set of experiments.

00:03:37.948 --> 00:03:39.630
And the tracks can be
divided into sections,

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.710
to run several different tests at once.

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.550
One of the tracks is also watertight,

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.570
for testing how pavements behave
on wet or soaked ground.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:50.670
And I really hope that the
size and scale of this thing

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.120
is coming across on camera,

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:55.830
because I've had to change my microphone
to the windproof one,

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:59.070
because of how much turbulent air
this thing generates.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:01.570
Those arms are twice my
height. They are massive.

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:05.450
It is genuinely unnerving
to be standing here,

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:09.460
not able to see this with
just this noise behind me.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:12.900
- There are some limitations, of course,
because it's an accelerated test.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.350
We cannot control the climate conditions.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:18.220
And then there is ageing of
the bituminous materials.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.840
They become more brittle.
And obviously we cannot simulate that,

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.600
because we have to do the
test in several months.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.970
We include one reference section
with a material that we know.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:30.710
We can make a comparison
with a classical material,

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:33.070
and correct the results
by some coefficients,

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:34.790
to adapt to real conditions.

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.120
- It's tempting to think
that in the 21st century,

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:40.060
we can test everything by
computer modelling and simulation.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:43.530
But those simulations have to be
grounded in truth somewhere.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:46.180
You can't build those models
without real-world data.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:47.710
And when you're dealing
with public safety

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:50.360
and billion-euro infrastructure projects,

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.473
sometimes there is no substitute
for just building something real.

00:04:55.648 --> 00:04:57.236
[carousel whirring]

